## The Real Unit We Stock: One Kramer VP-731, 9 Inputs

A video switcher, as a general term, covers everything from a small HDMI matrix box to a full broadcast vision mixer with camera inputs and a preview bus. What MEG actually stocks under that name is one specific product: a Kramer VP-731, a 9 input video scaler and switcher. We own exactly one unit.

We are being precise here on purpose. Our equipment catalog confirms the sources this unit has actually been used with on real bookings: a laptop for slides, a second laptop for a supporting video, and a DVD player for a screened clip, drawn from an LG-RH7500 DVD recorder and hard disk unit also in our inventory. There is no document camera or visualizer in our own inventory, and we will not improvise a substitute from what we do own. [Video Switcher vs Camera Switcher](#video-switcher-vs-camera-switcher-the-question-that-matters-most) below covers how we handle a request for one.

<InlineCTA />

## What 9 Inputs Actually Means in Practice

The VP-731's 9 physical inputs are a real spec, and we are not going to inflate what most events actually do with them. A corporate event rarely needs more than 2 or 3 presentation sources on a single screen, typically a laptop for the main presentation, a second laptop for a video insert, and occasionally a DVD player for a screened clip. That is the real, honest use case behind this device, not a 9 way simultaneous feed.

The headroom exists because the unit supports it, not because we expect a booking to fill all 9 inputs. What our technician does with that headroom is switch cleanly between whichever 2 or 3 sources your event actually has, so the room's screen cuts from one to the next without a visible glitch or a reboot. If your event genuinely has more than 3 sources to manage, [contact us](/contact/) with the detail and we will confirm whether it is realistic on the day.

## Video Switcher vs Camera Switcher: The Question That Matters Most

This is the single most important thing on this page. A video switcher, in the presentation sense that MEG offers, and a camera switcher or vision mixer built for live streaming and broadcast production, are two different categories of equipment used for two different jobs, and they get confused constantly in general searches for this term.

What our Kramer VP-731 does: it takes presentation sources, a laptop, a second laptop, a DVD player, and cuts the room's own screen cleanly between them, managed by our technician during your event.

What it does not do: it does not accept camera inputs, it does not produce a program feed for a live stream, and it has no preview or record output for broadcast use. Cameras, filming and live streaming are not part of our own inventory, and our own crew does not run them. A filmed record, a livestream to remote attendees or a multi camera broadcast mix is a different category of equipment and crew entirely. If your event needs one of those, or a document camera, describe it to us. We can see whether a supplier we work with might take it on, and if none will, we will say so plainly. Ask us directly, too, if you are unsure which category your event needs.

## Video Switcher or a Single Presentation Source: What Changes

Not every event needs source switching. Here is what genuinely changes between a single laptop presentation and a booking that adds the video switcher:

| | Single Presentation Source | Video Switcher Added |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Sources on screen | One laptop, connected directly to the projector or display | A laptop, a second laptop and a DVD player, switched between as needed |
| Switching a source mid event | Manual, unplug and reconnect, with a visible black screen or a reboot | Our technician cuts between sources with no visible glitch or reboot |
| Who manages it | The presenter, or nobody | A technician, on site for the duration |
| Realistic use case | A single speaker, one slide deck, no video insert | A conference agenda with more than one presenter or a video segment inside a live presentation |
| Included in a package | Yes, on any package with a screen | No, separate quote on request item |

If your event is one speaker with one slide deck, a single source is genuinely simpler and nothing here changes for you. If your agenda moves between two laptops, or a slide deck and a screened video clip, the switcher removes the visible fumble of reconnecting a cable live in front of your audience.

## Which Packages This Is Available On, and Pricing

None of our five packages, [Eco Pack](/packages/eco/), [Product Presentation Pack](/packages/product-presentation/), [Basic MICE Pack](/packages/basic-mice/), [Wedding Pack](/packages/wedding/) or [MICE Pack](/packages/mice/), include the video switcher as a standard inclusion. It only makes practical sense on a booking that already has a screen and more than one presentation source, which in our catalog means the corporate packages:

-   **[MICE Pack](/packages/mice/)**, with its 60 inch LED display screen and dedicated live technician included as standard, is where this device gets requested most, for a corporate agenda with more than one speaker or a video insert inside a keynote.
-   **[Basic MICE Pack](/packages/basic-mice/)**, with its 2x2m projection screen, can add the video switcher, but it requires the on site live technical assistant add on, because this is technician operated equipment, not a self run box you plug in yourself.

The [Eco Pack](/packages/eco/) has no screen included and is built for a self run DJ or party setup, and the [Wedding Pack](/packages/wedding/) does not include a projection screen either, so the video switcher genuinely does not fit either of those two. There is no published a la carte price for the switcher itself. [Contact us](/contact/) with your package, agenda and how many presentation sources your event actually has, and we will confirm availability and a quote rather than make one up here.

